{"uncertainty": 0.15, "outcome": "rtl_bug", "artifacts": [{"kind": "root_cause_analysis", "title": "Root cause: uart_tx reloads its hold register while a frame is in flight", "summary": "Root cause (rtl_bug): the approved RTL [artifact:prj-d4a591eb4b.design-under-investigation.a1] contains an unconditional 'if (start) hold <= data;' placed before the state case statement. A start pulse during START, DATA or STOP therefore overwrites the byte being shifted out. Every later data bit is then read from the new byte, through tx <= hold[bit_idx + 1]. This contradicts the module's own header contract: 'A start pulse while a frame is in flight is ignored.' The IDLE branch already loads hold correctly, so the extra assignment is redundant on the legal path and harmful on the busy path. The failure was reproduced upstream: 1 of 4 checks failed and $finish was reached [evidence:prj-d4a591eb4b.triage.e2] [run:run-0002]. That reproduction matches a single data-corruption check, not a hang or crash. Ranked hypotheses: (1) rtl_bug, hold reloaded while busy. This is the leading hypothesis, supported by the RTL text and by one failure out of four checks. It would be refuted if the new testbench passed on this RTL, or if tx still carried the original byte after a mid-frame start. (2) testbench_bug, a wrong expectation in the upstream repro. This is rejected: the expected behaviour is written in the RTL's own header, and the new independent repro checks only that documented contract. It would be refuted outright if a frame with no interference also failed, but here the clean-frame checks are designed to pass. (3) infrastructure. This is rejected: the upstream run built, reached $finish at 546000 and reported a check verdict [run:run-0002]. It would be supported by a build failure, a timeout, or a missing verdict. (4) spec_ambiguity. This is rejected as the primary cause, because the header comment states the busy-start behaviour explicitly. It remains a residual risk because no separate specification document was available to me. Triage's 30%-confidence 'Likely RTL bug' hypothesis [evidence:prj-d4a591eb4b.triage.e1] [run:run-0001] agrees with this conclusion, but it was weak on its own. This analysis rests mainly on the RTL text. Proposed fix: delete the standalone 'if (start) hold <= data;' and keep only the load inside IDLE. Traces to [requirement:req-d10c4df532]."}], "files": [{"path": "tb_uart_tx_reload_while_busy.v", "kind": "testbench", "title": "Self-checking repro: start pulse during a busy frame must be ignored", "summary": "Self-checking testbench for uart_tx (CLKS_PER_BIT=4) from [artifact:prj-d4a591eb4b.design-under-investigation.a1]. It runs 5 checks. First, a clean frame 0xA5: the received byte and the stop bit are checked. Second, a frame 0x0F with a start pulse carrying 0xF0 injected during the data bits: the received byte must still be 0x0F, and the stop bit is checked. Finally, the testbench confirms that no extra frame follows the ignored start. On the approved mutant, the interfered frame is expected to arrive corrupted (about 0xF7), giving 1 failed check. It prints a single NIRMAAN-SIM verdict with the run nonce before $finish, and includes a watchdog. Serves [requirement:req-d10c4df532].", "entry": "tb_uart_tx_reload_while_busy"}], "tool_runs": ["run-0001", "run-0002"], "claims": ["On a corrected RTL (with the standalone hold reload removed), the new testbench is expected to report 5 passed, 0 failed. This has not been run.", "On the approved mutant RTL, the new testbench is expected to report 4 passed, 1 failed, with the corrupted byte near 0xF7. This has not been run; the platform's simulator.run will decide."], "escalation": null, "notes": "Conclusion: rtl_bug. The upstream repro [run:run-0002] showed a clean single-check failure, and the RTL text shows hold being written whenever start is high, regardless of state. The independent review requirement is still open: review must be recorded by someone other than this seat after the platform's repro gate runs. The only statement of intended behaviour is the RTL header comment. If a separate spec says a busy start should be queued or reloaded, the outcome would change to spec_ambiguity."}

=== FILE: tb_uart_tx_reload_while_busy.v ===
`timescale 1ns / 1ps
module tb_uart_tx_reload_while_busy;
    reg        clk   = 1'b0;
    reg        rst   = 1'b1;
    reg        start = 1'b0;
    reg  [7:0] data  = 8'd0;
    wire       tx;
    wire       busy;

    uart_tx #(.CLKS_PER_BIT(4)) dut (
        .clk(clk), .rst(rst), .start(start), .data(data), .tx(tx), .busy(busy)
    );

    always #5 clk = ~clk;

    integer passed = 0;
    integer failed = 0;
    integer k;
    integer lows;
    reg [8*64-1:0] nonce;
    reg        done = 1'b0;
    reg  [7:0] b1, b2;
    reg        s1, s2;

    task send(input [7:0] d);
        begin
            @(negedge clk);
            data  = d;
            start = 1'b1;
            @(negedge clk);
            start = 1'b0;
        end
    endtask

    task recv(output [7:0] b, output stopok);
        integer i;
        begin
            @(negedge tx);
            repeat (2) @(negedge clk);       // mid start bit
            for (i = 0; i < 8; i = i + 1) begin
                repeat (4) @(negedge clk);   // mid data bit i
                b[i] = tx;
            end
            repeat (4) @(negedge clk);       // mid stop bit
            stopok = (tx == 1'b1);
        end
    endtask

    initial begin
        if (!$value$plusargs("nirmaan_nonce=%s", nonce))
            nonce = "none";
        repeat (3) @(negedge clk);
        rst = 1'b0;
        repeat (2) @(negedge clk);

        // Frame 1: clean transmission of 0xA5
        fork
            send(8'hA5);
            recv(b1, s1);
        join
        if (b1 == 8'hA5) passed = passed + 1;
        else begin failed = failed + 1; $display("FAIL: clean frame got %h expected a5", b1); end
        if (s1) passed = passed + 1;
        else begin failed = failed + 1; $display("FAIL: clean frame stop bit low"); end

        wait (!busy);
        repeat (4) @(negedge clk);

        // Frame 2: 0x0F with a start pulse (data 0xF0) during the data bits
        fork
            begin
                send(8'h0F);
                repeat (10) @(negedge clk);
                data  = 8'hF0;
                start = 1'b1;
                @(negedge clk);
                start = 1'b0;
                data  = 8'd0;
            end
            recv(b2, s2);
        join
        if (b2 == 8'h0F) passed = passed + 1;
        else begin failed = failed + 1; $display("FAIL: busy-start corrupted frame: got %h expected 0f", b2); end
        if (s2) passed = passed + 1;
        else begin failed = failed + 1; $display("FAIL: interfered frame stop bit low"); end

        // The ignored start must not launch another frame
        wait (!busy);
        lows = 0;
        for (k = 0; k < 60; k = k + 1) begin
            @(negedge clk);
            if (tx !== 1'b1) lows = lows + 1;
        end
        if (lows == 0) passed = passed + 1;
        else begin failed = failed + 1; $display("FAIL: extra frame after ignored start"); end

        done = 1'b1;
        $display("NIRMAAN-SIM %0s %0d passed, %0d failed", nonce, passed, failed);
        $finish;
    end

    initial begin
        #200000;
        if (!done) begin
            done = 1'b1;
            failed = failed + 1;
            $display("FAIL: watchdog timeout");
            $display("NIRMAAN-SIM %0s %0d passed, %0d failed", nonce, passed, failed);
            $finish;
        end
    end
endmodule
=== END FILE ===
